    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks Ben. This solved the phi smaller than 0 warnings, and the divergent transition warnings disappeared too. <div><br></div><div>I also figured that</div><div><br></div><div>1) I need to use neg_binomial_2 instead of neg_binomial_2_log since I&#39;m using mu,</div><div>2) since &#39;b&#39; can be negative, mean of NB can get negative too, so I changed to formulation a bit</div><div><br></div><div><div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>data { </div><div>  int&lt;lower=1&gt; N;</div><div>  int Y[N];</div><div>  int&lt;lower=1&gt; K;</div><div>  vector[N] X;</div><div>} </div><div>transformed data { </div><div>} </div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>parameters {</div><div>  real b;</div><div>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; shape;</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; mu0;</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><br></div><div>  real&lt;lower=0, upper = 1&gt; alpha;</div><div>  real&lt;lower=0, upper = 1 - alpha&gt; phi;</div><div>}</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>transformed parameters { </div><div>  vector[N] mu;</div><div>  real lambda;</div><div>  mu[1] &lt;- Y[1];</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>  lambda &lt;- 1 - alpha - phi;</div><div>  </div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>  for (t in 2:N) {</div><div>    mu[t] &lt;- exp(X[t] * b) * (lambda * mu0 + phi * mu[t - 1] + alpha * Y[t - 1]);</div><div>  }</div><div>} </div><div>model {</div><div>  alpha ~ normal(0.5, 0.5);</div><div><br></div><div>  Y ~ neg_binomial_2(mu, shape);</div><div>  </div><div>}</div></div><div><br></div><div>Any thoughts on these changes?</div><div><br></div><div>But still lambda becomes really small, which results in really large mu values. Will reparametrizing this using poisson-gamma decomposition help?</div><div><br></div><div>Also, what prior should I use for shape? Shape should be always positive, that&#39;s why I used gamma.</div><div><br></div><div>I get a few of these warnings too:</div><div><br></div><div>             Exception thrown at line 32: neg_binomial_2_log: Precision parameter is inf, but must be finite!     1<br></div><div><br></div><div>And how can I exclude Y[1]? Rhat value for mu[1] is NaN. This shouldn&#39;t cause problems, right?</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><br></div><div><br><br>On Wednesday, June 22, 2016 at 10:58:34 AM UTC-7, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Tuesday, June 21, 2016 at 6:30:30 PM UTC-4, Alireza RG wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">Here&#39;s the model I&#39;m trying to create:</font><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></div><div>\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\r\n\t\t<div title=\"Page 1\">\r\n\t\t\t<div>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t<p><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">z<span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">t </span>∼NB(μ<span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">t</span>, k),   μ<span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">t </span>=Xb + φμ<span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">t</span><span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">−</span><span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">1 </span>+αz<span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">t</span><span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">−</span><span style=\"vertical-align:-1pt\">1 </span>+(1−φ−α)μ </font></p>\r\n\t\t\t\t</div>\r\n\t\t\t</div>\r\n\t\t</div></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\"><br></font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">where X is my design matrix, b is fixed effect, k is shape, α, φ, μ are positive, and  α + φ &lt; 1.</font></div><div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\"></font></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br>You need to constrain the parameters such that alpha + phi &lt; 1. So, this<br> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">parameters { </font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">  real b;</font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">  real&lt;lower=0&gt; shape;</font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">  real&lt;lower=0&gt; mu;    </font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">  real&lt;lower=0&gt; alpha;</font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">  real&lt;lower=0&gt; tmp;</font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\" size=\"2\">} </font></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br>needs to become<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> shape</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> upper </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> alpha</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> upper </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> alpha</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>which does not have the tmp variable. Then you can push what you had for transformed parameters into the model block<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real </span><span style=\"color:#008\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// log(Y[1]) could be calculated in transformed data</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> alpha </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">lambda</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> alpha </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  Y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> neg_binomial_2_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> shape</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// this should really be excluding Y[1]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  alpha </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// don&#39;t use a gamma(0.01, 0.01) prior for shape</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><br>Ben<br><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Aearg66yBgAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-06-22T19:43:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: Negative Binomial model with damped dynamic mean",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Wednesday, June 22, 2016 at 3:29:17 PM UTC-4, Alireza RG wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I also figured that<div><br></div><div>1) I need to use neg_binomial_2 instead of neg_binomial_2_log since I&#39;m using mu,</div><div>2) since &#39;b&#39; can be negative, mean of NB can get negative too, so I changed to formulation a bit</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>neg_binomial_2 with log(mu) is better numerically.<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">But still lambda becomes really small, which results in really large mu values. Will reparametrizing this using poisson-gamma decomposition help?</div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>I doubt it. Perhaps you have stronger prior beliefs about alpha than standard uniform?<br><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>Also, what prior should I use for shape? Shape should be always positive, that&#39;s why I used gamma.</div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>The gamma distribution is decent but not with very small shape and rate hyperparameters. Figure out what mean and mode seem reasonable and then back out what the shape and rate should be.<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>I get a few of these warnings too:</div><div><br></div><div>             Exception thrown at line 32: neg_binomial_2_log: Precision parameter is inf, but must be finite!     1<br></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>One instance of that is nothing to worry about.<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div></div><div>And how can I exclude Y[1]? Rhat value for mu[1] is NaN. This shouldn&#39;t cause problems, right?</div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>The problem is that you are doing mu[1] &lt;- Y[1] and then modeling Y[1] with mu[1].  You could either put a marginal prior on mu[1] or omit Y[1] from the likelihood.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
